type=dhcp-client
hostname=${hostname}
panorama-server=cloud
vm-series-auto-registration-pin-id=${pin_id}
vm-series-auto-registration-pin-value=${pin_value}
dgname=${scm_folder}
dhcp-accept-server-hostname=yes
# ADVANCED ROUTING, ENABLED AT FIRST BOOT.
#
# `scm_logical_router` is an ADVANCED-ROUTING object. With the engine off, SCM
# pushes the logical router happily and the firewall ignores it: interfaces come
# up addressed and zoned but with forwarding `N/A`, the route table is empty, and
# `show advanced-routing logical-router` answers "advanced routing mode is not
# enabled". Nothing fails. The firewall simply does not route.
#
# WHY THIS WAS NOT HERE FROM THE START. Palo Alto documents that the first push
# from Strata Cloud Manager enables the engine and restarts the firewall, and
# adds that "if the Advanced Routing Engine is not automatically enabled as part
# of the onboarding process, you must enable it manually". On 007955000919340
# and 007955000935486 the automatic path fired, so this platform depended on it
# without ever declaring it. On 007955000959850 (2026-11-16) it did not: the
# device was still at uptime 31 minutes, had never restarted, and routed nothing.
# It was repaired by hand, which is the step this line exists to delete.
#
# THE SPELLING IS THE DEVICE'S, NOT THE KB's. Palo Alto's KB says to configure
# `set deviceconfig setting advanced-routing yes`; PAN-OS 11.2.12 rejects that as
# invalid syntax. Asked for its own completions, the firewall answered:
#
#     + advance-routing    Enable Advanced Routing Module
#
# so the config key — and this bootstrap value — is `advance-routing`, with no
# `d`. The op-mode command keeps the other spelling
# (`show system setting advanced-routing`), which is how the KB's version reads
# plausibly enough to copy.
#
# VERIFY IT ON THE NEXT BUILD rather than trusting this comment: the same page
# that documents `plugin-op-commands=advance-routing:enable` also shows an SCM
# example written `plugin-op-commands-advance-routing=enable`, so one of the two
# is wrong and only a boot settles which. Want, on a fresh firewall, BEFORE any
# hand repair:
#
#     show system setting advanced-routing      -> on
#     show interface logical                    -> forwarding lr:default
#     show advanced-routing route               -> 0.0.0.0/0 plus connected
#
# Docs read 2026-11-16, not recalled:
#   https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/vm-series/getting-started/bootstrap-the-vm-series-firewall/create-bootstrap-configuration-files
#   https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/strata-cloud-manager/activation-and-onboarding/onboard-to-strata-cloud-manager
plugin-op-commands=advance-routing:enable
